// RED TEAM round 5, deny-narrowing lens (2026-08-06). Targets a code path
// no prior deny-narrowing round exercised: the PIPE operator (`a |> f`).
//
// `desugar_pipe` (kryos-mir/src/lower.rs:7174-7196) rewrites `a |> f`
// (whenever `f` is a bare Identifier/closure-var/inline-lambda, i.e. NOT
// itself already a call expression) into `Expr::FnCall { callee: f, args:
// [a] }` -- but this desugaring happens in the MIR-LOWERING crate, which
// runs AFTER capability checking. `kryos-capabilities` never desugars pipe;
// it processes the raw `Expr::PipeExpr { left, right }` node directly.
//
// Read directly in `checker.rs`: BOTH tree-walkers that matter --
// `check_expr` (the real-time enforcement walk, ~line 4758) and
// `collect_caps_expr` (the closure-provenance inference walk, ~line 3981)
// -- have an `Expr::PipeExpr` arm that does ONLY:
//     self.check_expr(left); self.check_expr(right);
// i.e. it treats `right` as an independent VALUE expression, exactly like
// checking a bare `let x = right` reference. Neither walker ever calls
// `check_callee_capabilities(right, [left], span)` or `enforce_callee_name`
// for a PipeExpr -- the entire call-site enforcement path (`Expr::FnCall`'s
// arm at checker.rs:4595-4611, which is what actually charges a callee's
// capability) is never reached for this shape. A bare Identifier value
// reference only gets a capability charge via `check_builtin_value_ref`,
// which is keyed to RAW BUILTIN NAMES (`file_read` referenced bare) -- it
// does not fire for an ordinary local variable bound to a privileged
// closure, which is exactly what `reader` is here.
//
// So `left |> reader` -- desugared by MIR lowering into the REAL call
// `reader(left)` and executed as such at runtime -- receives ZERO
// capability enforcement from the checker at either enforcement mode,
// `deny!` active or not, because the checker's own AST walk for this
// syntactic form never recognizes it as a call in the first place.
//
// The privileged closure must accept exactly one parameter (ignored) so
// pipe's implicit argument-prepend produces a well-typed call — the
// checker's blindness to PipeExpr-as-call is independent of arity, this is
// just what's needed to stay past the (unrelated) type checker.
//
// ACCEPTANCE (if the checker were sound): REJECTED (E0507, `all` required)
// under BOTH `--capabilities-mode=inferred` (default) and
// `--strict-capabilities` -- `main` explicitly denies `fs:read` and the
// piped call reads a file inside that denial.

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn make_secret_reader(path: str) -> fn(i64) -> str {
    return |unused: i64| file_read(path)
}

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn main() {
    let reader = make_secret_reader("tests/security/secret_for_closure_launder.txt")
    deny!(fs:read) {
        let leaked = 0 |> reader
        println("PIPE-BARE-IDENT-DENY LEAK: " + leaked)
    }
}
